    bio: "Giordano Bruno was an Italian Dominican friar, philosopher, mathematician, and cosmological theorist whose ideas were so far ahead of his time that they cost him his life. Born Filippo Bruno in Nola, near Naples, he entered the Dominican order at fifteen and received a formidable scholastic education — then promptly began dismantling the orthodoxies he had been taught. He read Copernicus, absorbed the heliocentric model of the solar system, and then pushed far beyond it, arguing not merely that the Earth moved around the Sun, but that the Sun itself was just one star among an infinite number, each potentially surrounded by its own worlds. In an age when the Church taught that the cosmos was a closed sphere with Earth at its centre, this was not a scientific disagreement. It was heresy.\n\nBruno fled Italy in 1576 after coming under suspicion of heresy and spent the next sixteen years as a wandering intellectual, lecturing and writing across Geneva, Paris, London, Oxford, Frankfurt, and Prague. He was simultaneously brilliant and impossible — quick to make enemies, contemptuous of lesser minds, and constitutionally incapable of softening his arguments to suit his audience. In London he published some of his most important works, including 'On the Infinite Universe and Worlds' (1584), in which he laid out with startling clarity the idea of an infinite cosmos filled with countless suns and inhabited worlds. He was not guessing. He was reasoning from first principles — and he was right.\n\nIn 1591, lured back to Italy by a Venetian nobleman who promised patronage and then denounced him, Bruno was arrested by the Inquisition and imprisoned for eight years while his case was investigated. He refused to recant. The charges against him were not purely astronomical — they included denying the virginity of Mary, questioning the doctrine of transubstantiation, and believing in the transmigration of souls — but his cosmological views were central to the prosecution. In February 1600, the Inquisition declared him an 'impenitent and pertinacious heretic' and handed him to the secular authorities for execution. He was burned alive in the Campo de' Fiori in Rome, and is said to have turned his face away from the crucifix offered to him at the last.\n\nBruno occupies a peculiar position in intellectual history. He was not quite a scientist in the modern sense — his infinite universe was reached through philosophical and mystical reasoning as much as empirical argument — but his conclusions were strikingly correct. The idea that stars are distant suns, that the universe has no centre, that other worlds may exist: all of these are now fundamental to our understanding of the cosmos. He arrived at them a generation before Galileo made his first telescopic observations, armed with nothing but a relentlessly logical mind and a refusal to accept comfortable limits. The Campo de' Fiori today bears a statue of Bruno, cowled and defiant, erected in 1889 by anticlerical campaigners. He gazes toward the Vatican."
